WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.046 --> 00:00:10.085
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome to Superstars in History , the talk show featuring interviews with famous people from

2
00:00:10.086 --> 00:00:22.508
the past. I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was a fabulous scientist and inventor whose best-known invention is something that I personally could never live without.

3
00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:46.027
Please welcome the father of the telephone, Alexander Graham Bell!

4
00:00:46.028 --> 00:00:48.694
ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: Thank you for

5
00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:53.342
calling, Faye. FAYE MOSS: And thank you for

6
00:00:53.343 --> 00:00:59.842
inventing the telephone, Alexander! You were always interested in sound and communication, even as

7
00:00:59.843 --> 00:01:05.989
a child, right? ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: Yes, that's correct. I was

8
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:12.798
born in Scotland in 1847. My father was a speech teacher, so I grew up learning about all the sounds the

9
00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:21.412
human voice can make. And I loved to apply my knowledge in amusing ways—I was quite a good mimic, and I taught our

10
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:27.028
terrier to speak. FAYE MOSS: Excuse me? You taught your dog

11
00:01:27.029 --> 00:01:33.064
to say actual words? ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: While he growled, I moved his throat and mouth around to make it

12
00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:41.217
sound as if he were speaking. He could "say" things like, "How are you,

13
00:01:41.218 --> 00:01:48.048
Grandmama?" Visitors adored this trick! But on a much more serious note, my interest in sound also arose

14
00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:55.043
from my mother's situation. She was a gifted pianist, so I was greatly distressed when she started to

15
00:01:55.044 --> 00:01:59.578
lose her hearing. I was always dreaming of ways to improve

16
00:01:59.579 --> 00:02:07.375
her hearing. FAYE MOSS: It sounds like you were an imaginative, sensitive kid. Did you invent

17
00:02:07.376 --> 00:02:13.942
anything in childhood? ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: At age 12 I invented a de-husking machine for a neighbor

18
00:02:13.943 --> 00:02:19.122
who owned a flour mill. It quickly removed the husk from the wheat, saving a

19
00:02:19.123 --> 00:02:24.410
great amount of time. And my brother and I created a talking machine that was shaped like

20
00:02:24.411 --> 00:02:28.438
a human skull. It had rubber cheeks and lips and a

21
00:02:28.439 --> 00:02:34.094
tin voice box. To make the machine talk, we pumped air through a tube that served as

22
00:02:34.095 --> 00:02:38.756
its windpipe. It was clever, but I'm afraid it wasn't particularly

23
00:02:38.757 --> 00:02:50.669
useful! FAYE MOSS: You attended university in Scotland, and in 1868, you began teaching the deaf in London while doing experiments with sound in your

24
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:57.152
spare time. But then in 1870, you suddenly moved to Canada.

25
00:02:57.153 --> 00:03:01.456
Why? ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: Sadly, my two brothers

26
00:03:01.457 --> 00:03:07.295
died from tuberculosis. I was ill too, so my parents wanted me to move to Canada for fresh

27
00:03:07.296 --> 00:03:13.212
country air. While I recovered, I continued my sound experiments and study of the

28
00:03:13.213 --> 00:03:20.639
human voice. By 1871, I was well enough to move to Boston, to train teachers in Visible Speech.

29
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.444
This was a writing system invented by my father to help the deaf and mute learn to speak. I

30
00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:29.452
also began teaching deaf students. Oh,

31
00:03:29.453 --> 00:03:37.093
and I fell in love and got married. My wife, Mabel, also happened to be deaf. FAYE MOSS:

32
00:03:37.094 --> 00:03:45.179
Your work on sound and speech was so personal; it probably influenced your ideas for your inventions. When

33
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.706
did you start working on the telephone? ALEXANDER GRAHAM

34
00:03:48.707 --> 00:03:56.072
BELL: Like other inventors in the 1870s, I was particularly fascinated with the idea of improving the telegraph.

35
00:03:56.073 --> 00:04:03.177
It was a popular device for sending messages, but only one message could be transmitted across the wire at a time. This

36
00:04:03.178 --> 00:04:08.511
made the service slow, yet demand for faster telegraphs was growing rapidly. Inventors realized

37
00:04:08.512 --> 00:04:12.947
this was a tremendous opportunity! FAYE MOSS:

38
00:04:12.948 --> 00:04:16.286
How did you think you could improve the telegraph? ALEXANDER

39
00:04:16.287 --> 00:04:25.554
GRAHAM BELL: I had hoped to invent a harmonic telegraph that could transmit sounds of different pitches across a single wire, to send multiple messages at

40
00:04:25.555 --> 00:04:30.054
once. But I didn't know enough about electricity to make my ideas work.

41
00:04:30.055 --> 00:04:35.808
So I found an assistant, an electrician and mechanic named Thomas Watson. FAYE

42
00:04:35.809 --> 00:04:40.219
MOSS: How did your telegraph turn into the

43
00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.592
telephone? ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: That was

44
00:04:42.593 --> 00:04:48.738
a lucky accident. One day in 1875, while we were working on the telegraph, a

45
00:04:48.739 --> 00:04:55.517
transmitter jammed. When Watson tried to fix it, a part vibrated, making a twang that traveled along the

46
00:04:55.518 --> 00:05:01.548
wire. This made me wonder about the possibility of transmitting the human voice across a wire.

47
00:05:01.549 --> 00:05:11.786
Intrigued, we experimented further. At last, in March of 1876, I spoke into the mouthpiece of our electric speaking device: "Mr.

48
00:05:11.787 --> 00:05:18.567
Watson, come here—I want to see you!" And he heard me! My voice

49
00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:24.665
had traveled across the wire and through a speaker at the other end! FAYE MOSS: Your

50
00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:35.601
telephone patent is one of most valuable ones in history—but there's some controversy over who really invented the telephone. ALEXANDER GRAHAM

51
00:05:35.602 --> 00:05:48.176
BELL: Ah, yes. Electronic devices were a very new technology, and several inventors, like Antonio Meucci and Elisha Gray, were also working on communication devices.

52
00:05:48.177 --> 00:05:51.917
We each had patents for similar technologies. The

53
00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:56.219
question of who invented what and when is complicated. In fact,

54
00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:06.772
hundreds of court cases were brought against my company, the Bell Telephone Company. In the end, though, my patents were upheld. FAYE MOSS: Let's

55
00:06:06.773 --> 00:06:17.306
get back to your achievements. In 1880 the French government awarded you the Volta Prize. What did you do next? ALEXANDER GRAHAM

56
00:06:17.307 --> 00:06:25.393
BELL: I set up Volta Laboratory, an institution for experiments and research to improve the lives of the deaf.

57
00:06:25.394 --> 00:06:34.360
I also invented the photophone, a communication device that transmits speech messages using light beams instead of wires. People always

58
00:06:34.361 --> 00:06:44.330
made a fuss about the telephone, but I suspected the photophone would be recognized one day as a rather important invention too. FAYE MOSS: I

59
00:06:44.331 --> 00:06:53.372
think your photophone paved the way for fiber optics, a technology we use today for all kinds of communication. ALEXANDER GRAHAM

60
00:06:53.373 --> 00:06:59.728
BELL: Aha! I was correct then! Now I must tell you about my other inventions.

61
00:06:59.729 --> 00:07:04.566
Did you know I invented a telephonic probe, or metal detector? I

62
00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:12.409
invented it when President Garfield was shot in 1881 and doctors couldn't locate the bullet in his body. Sadly, I

63
00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:17.421
couldn't find the bullet, and poor Garfield died. Oh, I also

64
00:07:17.422 --> 00:07:21.221
designed kites and glides. Perhaps you've heard

65
00:07:21.222 --> 00:07:29.728
of the hydrofoil—a boat with airfoils? My final patent was for a very fast one! FAYE MOSS: You were

66
00:07:29.729 --> 00:07:33.467
interested in a lot of different things! Before you go,

67
00:07:33.468 --> 00:07:40.152
would you like to see my modern telephone? It's pretty amazing! ALEXANDER GRAHAM BELL: This

68
00:07:40.153 --> 00:07:45.748
might surprise you, Faye, but I lost some interest in the telephone over time. In fact,

69
00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:53.692
I refused to keep one in my study—too distracting for a busy scientist! So no, thank you. FAYE MOSS: No

70
00:07:53.693 --> 00:07:58.268
problem! We'll let you get back to history now. ALEXANDER GRAHAM

71
00:07:58.269 --> 00:08:02.747
BELL:

72
00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:09.122
Good-bye! FAYE MOSS: Alexander Graham Bell never considered his inventions to be his

73
00:08:09.123 --> 00:08:16.278
most important achievements. Instead he believed teaching the deaf was the most meaningful job he

74
00:08:16.279 --> 00:08:20.871
ever had. He died at the age of 75 in

75
00:08:20.872 --> 00:08:28.983
1922. On the day of his burial, every phone in America stayed silent for one minute to honor him.

76
00:08:28.984 --> 00:08:38.827
I hope you enjoyed today's interview. You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History !

